A||||5||GodSend||Cooling pattern!?||||5||
Z||000000||GodSend||10-09-2001||12:28 AM||||Hrmm, I noticed this pattern in my engine cooling I never had before, and I am searching for answers.<P>Firstly, they way my 87 gt NORMALLY works is, it basically warms up to operating temperature (say a 3 or 4 ticks from the left on the temp gauge).  The needle will stay exactly there, except for heavy stop and go traffic (traffic jams and such) where the needle will reach almost half way across the scope of the gauge - almost directly down).  As soon as you start moving again, the needle climbs down to the normal point.  Never overheats, so no problems here.<P>Recently (in the last few days).  The cooling works much different.  On the highway, the temp climbs up to the almost halfway-traffic jam point - and then creeps down to the almost normal point.  It only does this once.  After this point, the needle stays almost constant, but not like before.  It tends to travel about a tick in either direction.  <P>All this is at a pretty steady speed, flat gound.  It seems to have some sort of rythm almost.  The car still never overheats, but it is definatly something I am going to nip right away.<P>Any ideas where to start looking? (no work has been done it recently - I am obviously going to have a look at the coolant tomorrow)  But besides that.  Thanks guys||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||10-09-2001||12:30 AM||||Get a new thermostat and install it before the old one goes by the wayside!||||reg||1
Z||000002||WampusCat||10-09-2001||08:23 AM||||If the coolant level on the overflow tank got low enough, it could have sucked a little air into the system.  An air bubble in the system can cause some erratic temps.||||reg||1
Z||000003||GodSend||10-10-2001||09:49 AM||||Looks like the coolent was right near the bottom of the overflow.  I topped it back up, but am still getting the same problem.<P>Is it necessary to burp the system now just incase some air got in?  I guess my next step is to buy a new therm.||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTZ34||10-10-2001||10:20 AM||||Yes you may want to burp it.  There are several variants of doing this but all are similar.  I have ramps so I back mine on the ramps.  Take out the thermostat and start it for a few minutes.  Obvious but remember to let it cool b4 opening the thermostat cover.  Next remove the thermostat cover and top it off, put the thermsostat back in and your done.  Always check the recovery bottle because as you've experienced, it is a PITA once it has sucked air.  BTW, when I do this I put the car on the ramps (or with the hind end up higher than the front) and let the car sit for a few hours so the refill process moves along faster.  Also, my cooling pattern is identical to yours and I'm in south Florida.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000005||GodSend||10-10-2001||11:43 AM||||Thanks guys.  I will give it a try tonight, and I might as well throw in a new term. while I am in there. I will let you guys know how it works out.||||reg||1
